# Line-specific help only (LeetCode practice)

When the user asks a question about **specific line(s)** or attaches a **code selection** from a practice / LeetCode file (skeleton, partial attempt, or `@file (start-end)`):

## Do

- Answer **only** what is needed for those lines (syntax, API name, type error, one-line fix).
- Explain **that** construct briefly if helpful (e.g. `contains` vs `has`, `num` vs `nums`).
- Stay within the **same method or block** unless the user explicitly asks to widen scope.

## Do not

- Provide the **full solution** or complete corrected method.
- Add the **rest of the algorithm** (“then you would loop…”, “here’s the full decode…”).
- Paste **reference implementations** from the spacer or solve the whole problem unprompted.
- “While we’re here” refactors or extra test cases.

## Widen scope only when

The user **explicitly** asks, e.g. “show the full solution”, “how do I finish this method?”, “does my whole approach work?”, or “walk me through the entire algorithm”.

## Example

**User:** `@LongestConsecutiveSequence.java (38-42)` — `seen.has` doesn’t compile.

**Good:** Use `seen.contains(nums[i] - 1)` — Java `Set` has no `has`; that’s Python-style.

**Bad:** Here is the complete O(n) longest consecutive sequence with the full while-loop and return.
